@ik666
When reviewing the MusicBrainz API part of the old `CoverArtArchiveUtil` class (now `MusicBrainzUtil` in this PR), I see that a lot of search value are ignored, or worst (fail the request).

**For release :**
https://musicbrainz.org/doc/MusicBrainz_API/Search#Release
`tid` for trackId -> ignored
`recording` for title -> ignored
only `date` is take into account by MusicBrainz.

`arid` is not their but can be : (the MBID of any of the release artists)
`artistname` is not their but can be : (part of the name of any of the release artists)

**MusicBrainzUtil :**
The `MusicBrainzUtil` class should use `com.universalmediaserver:musicbrainz-api`
example :
```
MusicBrainzAPIClient client = new MusicBrainzAPIClient("UMS/Version ( https://www.universalmediaserver.com )");
ReleaseSearchEndpoint search = client.release().search();
//can be added inline
search.with(title).withArtistName(artist);
//or not
search.withReleaseDate(date);
...
//set a limit or not
search.setLimit(10);

//get results
ReleaseSearchResultsSchema results = search.getResults();
if (results == null) {
//something wrong
} else if (results.getCount() == 0) {
//no result
} else {
//do something with results.getReleases()
}
```
Be aware that MusicBrainz expect no more than 1 request by second by IP.
https://musicbrainz.org/doc/MusicBrainz_API/Rate_Limiting

> For example: if your requests are coming in at 4 requests per second, we don't honour 25% of them and decline the other 75% - we decline 100% of them, until the rate drops to 1 per second or lower. 

`com.universalmediaserver:musicbrainz-api` has a rate limiter implemented that follow this, then you will never fall in a declined request for that.

Hello everyone!

When publishing a track, with the following code

```
let bitrate = 30_000_000;
let framerate: f64 = 60.0;

local_participant
    .publish_track(
        LocalTrack::Video(track.clone()),
        TrackPublishOptions {
            source: TrackSource::Camera,
            video_codec: VideoCodec::VP9,
            video_encoding: Some(VideoEncoding {
                max_bitrate: bitrate,
                max_framerate: framerate,
            }),
            dtx: false,
            red: false,
            simulcast: false,
            ..Default::default()
        },
    )
    .await
    .unwrap();
```

We determined that tracks were always getting limited to 30fps and approx 4mbps. After digging into the `publish_track` method it was determined that the user defined `video_encoding` parameters were getting thrown out in favor of the defaults.

This PR modifies the `compute_appropriate_encoding` to properly take into account the user defined encoding settings. After making this change we were able to reliably push 60fps @ 30mbps using VP8/VP9/AV1 and are no longer running into a cap.
